      "function": {
        "description": "Execute a shell command in the host machine's terminal. Commands run in the current working directory by default. Use `cwd` to select an existing directory inside it or one the sandbox grants; relative paths resolve from the working directory. Neither the directory nor shell state persists between calls. No interactive commands.\n\n`timeout` (default 60, range 1–60) is a yield window, not a kill deadline. A command that finishes returns stdout and stderr with an exit code and elapsed time; a running one returns output so far and a session ID. `background: true` returns a session ID immediately. Sessions do not survive Cake exit or resume.\n\nUse BashSession with the session ID to read output, list sessions, or kill a process group. When the shell exits, remaining children in its process group stop. For polling or watch commands and other long-running work, start with `background: true` and use BashSession rather than a long foreground wait; do not append shell `&`.\n\nGuidance:\n- File search: Bash (ls, find, tree) for directories, Read for contents\n- Content search: Use rg (ripgrep) via Bash\n- Read files: Use Read tool, not cat/head/tail\n- Edit files: Use Edit tool, not sed/awk\n- Write files: Use Write tool, not echo/cat\n- Do not prefix commands with cd; use `cwd` instead. `cwd` applies to one call, so provide it again when needed\n\nNote: Shell interprets backticks (`) and $() inside double-quoted arguments as command substitution.\n\n`reason` is an optional, untrusted self-report; the gate judges only the command. Supply it for state-changing, destructive-looking, or remote-effect commands, stating the intended effect. The policy gate receives each command as a standalone request with no history, so put any required check in the same command and tie it to the affected target (for example, gate a merge on a `gh pr view` mergeable/state check in that same command). It never authorizes a remote destructive command or replaces the guard it needs.\n\nDirect `bash`, `sh`, `zsh`, `dash`, `ksh`, `ksh93`, `ash`, `mksh`, or `pdksh` script invocations (bare or `/bin/`/`/usr/bin/` path, optional `--`) are inspected as one UTF-8 script up to 32 KiB within read-path grants. Missing, denied, or oversized scripts block; other forms and nested dependencies are not inspected. Scripts are untrusted evidence and may change; results name the collected path.\n",
