    {
      "function": {
        "description": "Read a file's contents as unnumbered text. Supports reading specific line ranges to avoid loading large files entirely.\n\nBehavior:\n- Returns the selected lines verbatim under a `File:` header and a `Lines X-Y/Z` range line. The header and footer are metadata, not file content; do not include them in Edit old_text.\n- Defaults to reading lines 1-500 (the configured read_default_end_line window; configurable in [limits]). Provide start_line/end_line for large files.\n- Returns a \"N more lines; continue with start_line=X\" note when the file extends beyond the delivered range; large files may report \"at least N more lines\" because the exact total is not read to the end of the file.\n- When read_max_output_bytes stops delivery, the output ends at a complete line and names the byte cap and the start_line to resume from. Configurable in [limits].\n- Truncates any line longer than the configured read_max_line_bytes cap with a \"... (line truncated to N bytes)\" marker, so a single huge line cannot exhaust the window. Configurable in [limits].\n- Rejects files with null bytes. Detection covers at least the first 8 KiB and all bytes read for the requested range; large ranged reads stop at a bounded point.\n- Rejects invalid ranges (line numbers below 1, start_line greater than end_line, or start_line past EOF) and reports the file's line count.\n- Rejects directory paths (use Bash with ls, find, or tree for directory listings).\n\nWarning: The body has no synthetic line-number prefixes. Ranged and truncated reads return only the requested region; use only exact returned lines as Edit old_text, and omit the Read header and footer. Content outside the returned lines will not match.\n",
        "name": "Read",
        "parameters": {
          "properties": {
            "end_line": {
              "description": "Last line to read (1-indexed, inclusive). Default: the configured read_default_end_line window (500 out of the box; start_line+window-1 when start_line given)",
              "minimum": 1,
              "type": "integer"
            },
            "intent": {
              "description": "Optional. One short, user-facing sentence in the present progressive describing what this call does, for example \"Reading the parser tests\". Shown to the user instead of the raw arguments.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "path": {
              "description": "Absolute path to the file to read",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "start_line": {
              "description": "First line to read (1-indexed, inclusive). Default: 1",
              "minimum": 1,
              "type": "integer"
            }
          },
          "required": [
            "path"
          ],
          "type": "object"
        }
      },
      "type": "function"
    },
    {
      "function": {
        "description": "Read an image file and return it to you as an image you can actually see. Supports PNG, JPEG, GIF, and WebP.\n\nBehavior:\n- Requires an absolute path to a file inside the working directory, a temp directory, or a directory added with --add-dir.\n- Detects the format from the file's leading bytes, not its extension. Files that are not images are rejected with a message pointing at Read for text files.\n- Rejects files larger than the configured read_image_max_bytes cap (default 5 MiB) with a clear error instead of sending them, so an oversized image never reaches the provider.\n- Returns the image inline as a base64 data URL; there is no upload step.\n- Downscales an image whose longest edge exceeds 2048 px before sending it, so a large screenshot or photo still reaches the provider. A downscaled PNG stays PNG and anything else becomes JPEG, except that transparency forces PNG; a downscaled GIF becomes a single frame. Images inside the cap are sent byte for byte.\n\nGuidance:\n- Use ReadImage when a screenshot, diagram, chart, or photo is part of the question; Read rejects binary files.\n- Prefer the smallest image that answers the question; a cropped region costs far fewer tokens than a full screenshot.\n",
        "name": "ReadImage",
        "parameters": {
